cartridge "TELEPORTER/C. M. BLAKEMORE/1986"

' TELEPORTER
' A ColecoVision game based on "Teleporter" by Cleveland M. Blakemore,
' Ahoy! November 1986 (Commodore 64, BASIC).
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a cell-for-cell port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' listing, orig/teleporter.bas), the game is built for the ColecoVision:
' a drawn teleporter chamber and image monitor, aliens as big doubled
' sprites that materialise row by row, a lock lamp, sound effects and an
' original title tune.
'
' The game: you run the teleporter of the U.S.S. Nefarius. Twenty-one
' visitors are beamed aboard, one after another. Before each transport
' the helm posts the expected BEAM and TARGET readings and a reference
' WAVE, and the image monitor shows who is expected. About half the
' visitors are phonies. While the visitor materialises, watch the live
' readings, the live wave, the lock lamp and the figure on the pad:
' a phony's signal drifts (readings off by more than 0.5, a wrong wave,
' a red lamp), and early on its shape or colour may not match the
' monitor. Press a button to abort and fry a phony. Frying a real
' visitor, or letting a phony aboard, is an error. From the fourteenth
' visitor on, the captain reports trouble: the computer on the fritz
' (no live readings), meteors and radiation storms (shaking, no live
' wave), lunchtime (no lamp), a garbled transmission (the monitor shows
' the wrong figure), energy saving (no live readings or wave). At the
' end you get a rating by your errors; more than six and you are fired.
'
' From the original: 21 transports (L = 0 to 20); a phony one time in
' two; the posted readings (BEAM 50.0-94.9, TARGET 65.0-94.9); the drift
' each step, EC = INT(RND * VI) + INT(RND * 8 * F) tenths with VI
' swinging between 5 and -5 (a real visitor stays within 0.5, a phony
' adds up to 0.7); the phony's shape differs when RND * 7 > L and its
' colour when RND * 12 > L; the five waveforms in pairs, the live one
' slipping to its twin; 43 beam steps; aborting at any step; the four
' outcomes and their messages (PHONY... GOOD JOB, YOU JUST FRIED THE
' <TYPE>!, ANOTHER ALIEN FREELOADER, GOOD JOB); the events of the last
' eight transports in the listing's order (ON L-12 GOSUB); the races and
' ranks of visitor; the rating (11 ranks by errors) and YOU'RE FIRED
' above six errors.
'
' Liberties: the posted readings stay on screen beside the live ones
' (the C64 printed the live ones over them). The live wave and the lamp
' slip when the drift is 0.6 or more (the listing's index 39.6 + W +
' ABS(EC) slipped on some steps of real visitors too, through rounding,
' and a red lamp at 0.5 was right for both); the readings are the same.
' One drift sample per beam step (the listing printed two), a step every
' 10 frames (about 7 seconds a transport); the button is read every
' frame instead of once a step. The screen shake is the aliens' sprites
' shaking, not the whole screen. A rating with more than 20 errors (the
' listing printed none) is RAW RECRUIT. The listing's two sprite
' layers (multicolour) are a body and a detail sprite.
'
' Controls: either controller; a side button aborts the transport. On
' the title, up and down choose, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.
' Own xorshift random numbers. Nothing flashes faster than 3 Hz.
'
' Workarounds for Amy bugs telep-call-compare-clobber removed 2026-11-01.

